{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793745448,"nonce":"XxAk5agZ6pMW/LMVqXOv5dcAHsw=","title":"Interpreter: reduce per-frame GC root view and registry overhead","message":"Follow-up to 693a5fd2eef1a8a4f57b2cc3d0d95b6fd13a242c5ba029a5d6b118433e866778. Frame and argument buffer reuse removes 929 Rust allocations per warmed fib(12) call (465 interpreted frames), but root-enabled execution still makes 471 allocations versus 2 with roots disabled. FrameRoots owns a fresh boxed FrameView and inserts/removes a BTreeMap root entry for every active frame.\n\nNUC ordinary release measurements, 15 alternating rounds: recursive frames 157.62 ns without roots and 208.46 ns with roots after buffer reuse. This is not an isolated estimate of root registration cost or a whole-program prediction. A separate mixed roots-off/on perf run samples root registration at 5.05%, root BTreeMap insertion at 2.49%, removal at 2.49%, and remove_root_range at 2.42%. The per-native-call stack clear in 9a71873 is a separate issue.\n\nInvestigate stable reusable root views and a cheaper registry for active interpreter frames. Root addresses must stay valid across recursion and native callbacks; recycled frames must stop rooting old values on return, error and OSR transfer. Do not keep inactive frames registered. Measure cold calls, recursion, weak references/finalizers, and full tiered wall time before adopting a representation. Storage retention must remain bounded.\n\nEvidence: /private/tmp/zyntax-frame-storage/nuc/{micro.json,base-audit.log,candidate-audit.log,profile.txt,lua.json,python.json}. NUC i5-1250P, CPU affinity 0,2,4,6, no Cargo/rustc during timing. Separate allocation-audit build was used for allocation counts only.","files":null}]}